"The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) loved Surah al-A'la very much. He named this surah 'the most virtuous of the surahs describing the glorification of Allah.' According to a narration from Aisha, the Prophet would recite al-A'la in the first rak'ah of the witr prayer, al-Kafirun in the second, and al-Ikhlas along with the Mu'awwidhatayn in the third. In addition, he would recite Surah al-A'la and Surah al-Ghashiyah during the Eid and Friday prayers.\n\nAt the end of Surah at-Tariq, there was a promise that the Messenger of Allah would prevail against the plots of the disbelievers. In this surah, however, this promise is reinforced with the verse, 'We shall ease you unto the easiest way,' and guaranteed through the Lord's name 'al-A'la' (the Most High). Following this, an invitation is made to glorify the lofty name of the Lord, and it is emphasized that the Hereafter is better and more enduring.\n\nThe surah consists of 19 verses. In the first verse, it is commanded, 'Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High.' Tasbih (glorification) is to hold Allah free from any defective attributes. The name 'al-A'la' expresses that Allah is transcendent above all things in His Essence and attributes. Just as it is impermissible to bestow this name upon anyone else, it is likewise necessary to mention the names used for Allah with reverence, keeping them far from meanings unworthy of Him.\n\nGranting respite to the disbelievers at the end of the previous surah, along with the reminder of the Lord's majesty here, serves as an assurance that the promise will be fulfilled. The command to glorify is simultaneously an invitation to the duty of gratitude. Although there are different views regarding the word 'name' (ism), it is understood that the primary intent is to declare the transcendence of the Possessor of the name.\n\nWhen verse 74 of Surah al-Waqi'ah was revealed, the Prophet commanded saying it in bowing (ruku'), and when verse 96 was revealed, he commanded saying 'Subhana Rabbiya'l-A'la' in prostration (sujud). This shows that glorification can be performed through words and actions. Although it has also been suggested that what is meant by 'name' is the trace or work of creation, there is no need for this interpretation; because a name is the expression that identifies its possessor and is clearly understood.\n\nIn the second verse, it is stated that Allah created and proportioned, and in the third, that He determined and guided. Creation is a requirement of the name Rabb (Lord). Determining (taqdir) is to assign a measure and destiny to everything, while guidance (hidayah) is to direct them toward their purposes of creation. This also encompasses the guidance of intellect and religion given to humanity. All of these are the works of Allah's Lordship (Rububiyyah), and none other than He can accomplish them.\n\nIn the fourth verse, it is stated that it is Allah who brings forth the pasture, and in the fifth verse, that He turns it into black stubble like flood-scum. This points to plants drying up and decaying over time to turn into compost and coal. Attention is likewise drawn to the formation of coal. The end of those who seek humanity solely in material pleasures will also be like such a blackened heap.\n\nIn the sixth and seventh verses, it is stated that the Qur'an will be recited to the Messenger of Allah and that he will not forget, except what Allah wills. In the eighth verse, it is said, 'And We shall ease you unto the easiest way.' In the ninth verse, he is commanded to remind, if the reminder benefits. In the tenth and eleventh verses, it is stated that the one who has reverence will take heed, while the wretched one will avoid it.\n\nIn the twelfth and thirteenth verses, it is stated that the wretched will enter the greatest Fire, where he will neither die nor live. In the fourteenth verse, it is declared that whoever purifies himself will attain prosperity, and in the fifteenth, that whoever remembers the name of his Lord and prays will be saved. In the sixteenth and seventeenth verses, it is emphasized that people prefer the worldly life, whereas the Hereafter is better and more enduring. In the last two verses, it is recalled that these truths are also found in the scriptures of Abraham and Moses (Musa).\n\nAddressing His Prophet (peace be upon him) with the words 'We will make you recite,' Allah informs that He will reveal and teach the Qur'an through Gabriel (Jibril) and grant him success in reciting it. This recitation is in the sense of reciting from memory, and our Prophet will no longer forget. However, if Allah wills, He can cause him to forget; this demonstrates that His power cannot be restricted. This exception draws attention to the fact that the Prophet's not forgetting is not due to his own power, but to the grace of Allah. Scholars such as al-Farra have stated that this exception was actually intended to emphasize that forgetting would never occur at all. Indeed, Allah stated that if He willed, He could take away the revelation entirely, yet He did not will it. This promise is one of the miracles of the Prophet; occasional minor forgetfulness occurring for a divine wisdom does not harm the permanence of this promise.\n\nThen, by saying 'We shall ease you unto the easiest way,' Allah promises to grant His Prophet the easiest path in every matter. This signifies ease in knowledge, teaching, action, and guiding to truth. In every domain of religion, attaining even the most difficult goals with ease will be the characteristic of the Prophet and his community (ummah). The source of this ease is Allah's bestowal of success (tawfiq). Receiving both manifest and hidden revelation and comprehending the lenient rulings of the Shariah also fall within the scope of this ease.\n\nBy stating, 'Remind, if the reminder benefits,' Allah commands His Prophet to preach and advise in situations where reminding will be of benefit. This condition is not actually a restriction, but rather for emphasis and mitigation; for in reality, reminder inevitably provides benefit to the recipients. In some cases, however, reminding may increase the intransigence of stubborn disbelievers; in such circumstances, giving advice may not be obligatory. Those who show reverence take heed, while the most wretched avoid it. Those wretched ones will be cast into the great Fire, where they will neither die nor live.\n\nThe wretched who will be cast into the greatest Fire will suffer eternal punishment of Hell in the Hereafter. They will neither die and be relieved, nor will they find a beneficial life. This expresses the severity and permanence of the punishment. Sinful believers, however, will die after remaining in Hell for a time and will be saved through intercession (shafa'ah). For the disbelievers, there is no death; the torment is eternal.\n\nIn contrast, with the declaration, 'He has certainly succeeded who purifies himself,' whoever is cleansed of evils, purifies their heart from polytheism (shirk) and bad character, pays zakah, and performs prayer has attained salvation. Purification (tazakki) here encompasses both internal and external purification, such as faith (iman), sincerity (ikhlas), major ritual ablution (ghusl), and minor ritual ablution (wudu). Although some scholars have said that the prayer in this verse refers to the Eid prayer and zakah refers to the fitr charity (sadaqat al-fitr), these rulings may have come later since the revelation of the verse took place in Mecca. Essentially, the verse encompasses all bodily and financial acts of worship.\n\nYet instead of this purification and acts of worship that bring salvation, people prefer the life of this world. They prioritize the ornaments, amusements, and pleasures of the world and cast the Hereafter behind them. This preference is either the state of the disbelievers who reject the Hereafter entirely, or the state of heedless or disobedient believers who, despite believing in the Hereafter, prioritize this world. Yet the Hereafter is better and more enduring than this world.\n\nThese reminders were also present in the scriptures of earlier prophets. Especially in the scriptures of Abraham and Moses, there were fundamental principles such as declaring the oneness of Allah (tawhid), purifying oneself, performing prayer, and that the Hereafter is better than this world. This demonstrates that the religion of Islam carries the same fundamental message as previous religions. The Prophet being granted success in the easiest path and the triumph of his religion were also heralded in these scriptures."